        "\n",
        "Median-impute \"study_hours_reported\" -> The observed distribution is approximately symmetric (mean 10.49, median 10.50), so the median preserves the center without being sensitive to extremes.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Standardize housing into three groups -> Capitalization, spaces, and hyphenation represented the same three underlying housing groups.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Remove impossible ages -> Ages 0, 1, 3, -22, 199, and 220 cannot be trusted as college-student ages and cannot be corrected confidently from the row alone.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Remove negative work hours -> Negative hours are impossible, and there is no reliable information in the file for reconstructing the intended value.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Keep extreme but possible values -> Values such as age 39 and 40 work hours/week are unusual but possible, so they are not treated as errors.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "De-duplicate \"student_records\" by \"student_id\" -> The final table requires one roster record per student; repeated IDs are therefore reduced to one record.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Do not de-duplicate enrollments/activity -> Those files have one row per enrollment or week, so multiple rows per student are expected and meaningful.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Standardize IDs -> Numeric student IDs were extracted from `\"NU\" so the roster can join to the numeric enrollment key.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Aggregate enrollments before joining -> A direct enrollment join would create multiple rows per student; counts/distinct counts preserve one-row-per-student grain.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Aggregate weekly activity before joining -> Weekly observations must be summarized before joining to prevent row multiplication.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Parse mixed enrollment dates -> The raw file contains multiple valid date formats, so mixed-format parsing standardizes them without losing valid dates.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Z-score study hours -> Standardization makes the numeric variable comparable on a common mean-0, standard-deviation-1 scale.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Bin final GPA -> Three GPA bands make the continuous GPA variable easier to summarize and compare.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Retain 0.00 GPA values -> They are extreme but not established as impossible by the cleaning rules, so they remain in the analysis."
